## What changed

`Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:4` — `extractTags(from:)` no longer splits the body on spaces and requires each token to *start* with `#`. That was the bug: `"shopping list,#urgent"` produced the token `list,#urgent` (doesn't start with `#`), and `"(#ideas)"` produced `(#ideas)` (starts with `(`).

The new implementation uses an `NSRegularExpression` for `#\w+`, matching the `#` plus its word characters anywhere in the text regardless of surrounding punctuation. Because `matches` are returned left-to-right, tags come back in appearance order.

## Verified
- Compiled and ran against the actual `NoteKit.swift` (SwiftPM's sandbox can't run here, so I compiled the source + a harness with `swiftc` directly).
- Both reported examples now register their tags.
- Existing passing tests (`extractTagsSimple`, `extractTagsNoneWhenAbsent`) still return identical results, plus order-preservation for multiple tags.

One behavior note: `\w+` matches `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, so a tag like `#café` or `#naïve` would stop at the non-ASCII letter (yielding `café`/`na`). That's intentional — it mirrors the old space-delimited behavior where only ASCII word chars followed `#`. If you want Unicode letters in tags, I can switch the pattern to `#[\p{L}\p{N}_]+`.


